That's a human wizard, right?

What's your method for dispatching dungeon denizens? Spells or weapon?
Poking weaker critters with Magicbane works o.k., but there are too
many heavies in Gehennom to rely on it. Particularly because your AC
is mediocre.
If you're mostly using spells for combat, you probably don't need
gauntlets of power; carrying capacity maxes out around 18/10 strength
and 18 constitution, i think; additional strength only gives damage
boni to physical attacks.

> I just wasted a wish on 7 blessed wax candles.  (the first 7 were in a
> BoH where I accidentally put in another BoH).

It wasn't all that pressing to get them right now. You could have
sieved through Gehennom for more (detect treasure spell helps a lot).

> Important inv:
> 
> a blessed amulet of life saving (being worn)
> the blessed Eye of the Aethiopica
> the rustproof +0 Fire Brand
> the rustproof +0 Magicbane (weapon in hand)

If you can attain basic skill in long sword, Fire Brand would well be
worth enchanting, in spite of the relatively high number of fire
immunes in Gehennom. As long as the brand kicks in, every extra point
of enchantment equals two more points of damage. And it does its extra
damage against most dragons, most undead, angels and aligned priests.

> a blessed fireproof +2 pair of speed boots (being worn)
> a blessed greased rustproof +2 helm of brilliance (being worn)

On second thoughts, that's probably an orcish wizard. Enchant the
helmet first, look through armour stacks for pre-enchanted gauntlets
and maybe, as someone else suggested, a small shield. Anyway, enchant
as much as you can. AC is your friend - monsters will land fewer and
weaker hits. If you're mostly using spells, it's not all that vital,
but still very valuable: you can still get surrounded on a
no-teleport-no-dig level and AC will make your hitpoints last longer.

